1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:09,770
Welcome to Africa episode two of eight. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,425 --> 00:00:24,845
Eight countries take us from offshore Cabo Verde down the Atlantic coast:
Mauritania, Senegal, The Gambia, Guinea-Bissau, Guinea, Sierra Leone and
Liberia.

3
00:00:25,528 --> 00:00:29,150
Let's bring Cabo Verde and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:31,781 --> 00:00:42,869
Cabo Verde is an Atlantic island country west of Senegal. Its islands sit
offshore, separate from the mainland countries that follow the African coast.

5
00:00:43,522 --> 00:00:58,197
Cidade Velha on Santiago preserves a fortress, churches and an early colonial
street pattern. Its history connects these islands with Atlantic navigation
and the exploitation of enslaved people.

6
00:00:58,854 --> 00:01:09,129
Picture an old fortified town out in the Atlantic. Cabo Verde is the offshore
stop west of Senegal, not a coastal province on the mainland.

7
00:01:09,817 --> 00:01:13,742
Let's bring Mauritania and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:16,073 --> 00:01:24,467
Mauritania lies on the Atlantic below Western Sahara, north of Senegal and
west of Mali. Much of the country belongs to the Sahara.

9
00:01:25,131 --> 00:01:36,729
Desert towns including Chinguetti and Ouadane grew around caravan trade and
Islamic learning. Their narrow streets and courtyard houses preserve the shape
of Saharan trading settlements.

10
00:01:37,385 --> 00:01:49,843
Picture a caravan town between desert routes and the Atlantic. Mauritania is
above Senegal; Mali continues inland to its east.

11
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:53,676
Let's bring Senegal and its neighbours into view.

12
00:01:55,952 --> 00:02:05,507
Senegal lies below Mauritania at Africa’s western shoulder. It surrounds The
Gambia on land, while Guinea-Bissau and Guinea lie farther south.

13
00:02:06,172 --> 00:02:21,486
Stone circles in Senegal and The Gambia form a shared archaeological
landscape. Carefully shaped pillars and burial mounds record societies that
built sacred monuments over many centuries.

14
00:02:22,136 --> 00:02:32,028
Remember stone circles around the river country. Senegal wraps around The
Gambia, so the long narrow shape inside its western side is a separate
country.

15
00:02:34,317 --> 00:02:42,119
The Gambia is the narrow country following the River Gambia inland from the
Atlantic. Senegal surrounds it on every landward side.

16
00:02:42,769 --> 00:02:57,908
Kunta Kinteh Island and associated river sites preserve evidence of encounters
between Africa and Europe, including the slave trade and its abolition. The
river was a route into the interior.

17
00:02:58,568 --> 00:03:09,528
Use the river as your memory line: a thin country inside Senegal. The Gambia
reaches the Atlantic, although most of its outline looks like an inland
ribbon.

18
00:03:10,214 --> 00:03:13,581
Let's bring Guinea-Bissau and its neighbours into view.

19
00:03:17,012 --> 00:03:27,461
Guinea-Bissau lies below Senegal and northwest of Guinea. Its mainland coast
faces the Atlantic, with the Bijagós islands just offshore.

20
00:03:28,115 --> 00:03:40,398
The Bijagós archipelago has important coastal habitats and nesting beaches for
sea turtles. Local communities and customary resource management are central
to protecting this island environment.

21
00:03:41,059 --> 00:03:51,427
Picture turtle islands off the small coast below Senegal. Guinea-Bissau is
northwest of Guinea; their similar names belong to separate countries.

22
00:03:53,945 --> 00:04:05,369
Guinea lies below Guinea-Bissau and west of Mali, with Sierra Leone and
Liberia to its south. Its territory reaches the Atlantic despite its broad
inland part.

23
00:04:06,026 --> 00:04:18,646
Mount Nimba rises near the meeting of Guinea, Liberia and Côte d'Ivoire. Its
unusual wildlife includes a toad that gives birth to live young, rather than
laying eggs in a pond.

24
00:04:19,306 --> 00:04:29,070
Remember the live-bearing toad on the mountain border. Guinea is the larger
inland-reaching country above Sierra Leone and Liberia, not Guinea-Bissau.

25
00:04:31,369 --> 00:04:42,201
Sierra Leone is the Atlantic country southwest of Guinea and northwest of
Liberia. Its compact outline fits between those two neighbours.

26
00:04:42,853 --> 00:04:56,413
The Gola-Tiwai protected landscape preserves Upper Guinean rainforest and
river-island habitats. It shelters rare wildlife including the pygmy
hippopotamus, a much smaller relative of the familiar river hippo.

27
00:04:57,076 --> 00:05:07,270
Picture a small forest hippo in the compact coastal country. Sierra Leone
comes before Liberia when following this coast southeast.

28
00:05:09,531 --> 00:05:21,304
Liberia follows the Atlantic coast southeast of Sierra Leone, below Guinea and
west of Côte d'Ivoire. Its inland forests connect with the wider Upper Guinean
forest region.

29
00:05:21,958 --> 00:05:35,867
Sapo National Park in Sinoe County protects an important tract of tropical
forest. Liberia’s environmental authorities describe it as a major refuge for
the country’s biodiversity.

30
00:05:36,529 --> 00:05:47,106
Remember the forest refuge at the southeastern end of this coastal chain.
Liberia lies between Sierra Leone and Côte d'Ivoire, not inland behind them.

31
00:05:55,244 --> 00:06:09,408
Cabo Verde is offshore. Mauritania lies above Senegal, which surrounds The
Gambia on land. Guinea-Bissau is below Senegal, Guinea stretches inland, then
Sierra Leone and Liberia follow the coast southeast.

32
00:06:10,068 --> 00:06:24,906
Recall the old island town, caravan settlements, stone circles, river island,
turtle beaches, live-bearing toad, pygmy hippo and forest refuge. Use the
coastline to put the names back in order.
